\title{The Arithmetic of the Berggren Tree over $\Q(\sqrt{2})$:\\
Silver Units, the Boundary Hecke Algebra,\\ and Three Obstructions to Automorphy}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{21 August 2026}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
The Berggren (Barning--Hall) tree is the free ternary tree on the root $(3,4,5)$ generated by
three integral isometries $A,B,C$ of the Lorentz form $Q(a,b,c)=a^{2}+b^{2}-c^{2}$; it enumerates
every primitive Pythagorean triple exactly once. The middle generator has characteristic polynomial
$(X+1)(X^{2}-6X+1)$, whose hyperbolic eigenvalues $3\pm2\sqrt2$ are the square of the fundamental
unit $1+\sqrt2$ of $\Z[\sqrt2]$ and its inverse; the tree is $3$-regular, so its boundary is the
$3$-adic Cantor set and $q+1=3$ with $q=2$ the residue cardinality of the ramified prime
$(\sqrt2)$. These coincidences suggest that the boundary should carry automorphic forms for a
Hilbert modular group over $\Q(\sqrt2)$, with the silver units as Hecke eigenvalues. We prove that
this is false in three independent ways, and determine what is present instead. First, the
\emph{silver coordinate} $\zeta(a,b,c)=(a+b)+c\sqrt2$ intertwines the middle generator with
multiplication by $3+2\sqrt2$; on the light cone $N(\zeta)=-(a-b)^{2}$, and a node has unit silver
coordinate if and only if its address is a word in the single letter $B$, so the
$\Q(\sqrt2)$-locus of the boundary is a single point. Second, every element of the Berggren monoid
has rational integer trace, whereas the Hilbert modular group contains elements of trace
$1+\sqrt2$; the invariant trace field is therefore $\Q$. Third, the boundary Hecke (transfer)
operator $T$ and the shift $U$ satisfy $TU=3\,\mathrm{id}$ and $(UT)^{2}=3\,(UT)$, so the boundary
spectrum is exactly $\{0,3\}$; the units are not eigenvalues, the eigenvalue $3$ exceeds the
Ramanujan bound $2\sqrt{q}=2\sqrt2$, and $(3+2\sqrt2)(3-2\sqrt2)=1\neq q=2$, so the pair is not a
Satake pair. The mechanism is a spectral trichotomy of the alphabet: $A$ and $C$ are unipotent
parabolics fixing the rational cusps $(0,1,1)$ and $(1,0,1)$ with quadratically growing branches,
while $B$ is hyperbolic with quadratic irrational ideal endpoints $(1,1,\pm\sqrt2)$ and exponential
growth. The Berggren tree is a $\Q$-arithmetic object whose hyperbolic elements have real quadratic
multipliers: $\Q(\sqrt2)$ is an eigenvalue field, not a field of definition.
\end{abstract}

